  "estimatedMinutes": null,
  "statement": "Let \\(f(x)=x^{3}+3 x-1\\) have roots \\(a, b, c\\). Given that\n\n\\[\\frac{1}{a^{3}+b^{3}}+\\frac{1}{b^{3}+c^{3}}+\\frac{1}{c^{3}+a^{3}}\\]\n\ncan be written as \\(\\frac{m}{n}\\), where \\(m, n\\) are positive integers and \\(\\operatorname{gcd}(m, n)=1\\), find \\(100 m+n\\).",
  "answerChoices": [],
  "answer": {
    "letter": "3989",
    "value": "3989"
  },
  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.hmmt-nov-team-2021-p7.hmmt",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "We know that \\(a^{3}=-3 a+1\\) and similarly for \\(b, c\\), so\n\n\\[\\frac{1}{a^{3}+b^{3}}=\\frac{1}{2-3 a-3 b}=\\frac{1}{2+3 c}=\\frac{1}{3(2 / 3+c)} .\\]\n\nNow,\n\n\\[f(x-2 / 3)=x^{3}-2 x^{2}+\\frac{13}{3} x-\\frac{89}{27}\\]\n\nhas roots \\(a+2 / 3, b+2 / 3\\), and \\(c+2 / 3\\). Thus the answer is, by Vieta's formulas,\n\n\\[\\frac{1}{3} \\frac{(a+2 / 3)(b+2 / 3)+(a+2 / 3)(c+2 / 3)+(b+2 / 3)(c+2 / 3)}{(a+2 / 3)(b+2 / 3)(c+2 / 3)}=\\frac{13 / 3}{3 \\cdot 89 / 27}=\\frac{39}{89}\\]",
      "hints": [],
      "images": []
    }
  ],
